  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nOne more tranquil day is in store with partly sunny skies with \nhighs in the upper 70s to lower 80s. For tonight, clouds will be \non the increase with a chance of showers and isolated \nthunderstorms across the north. Instability is meager, thus severe\nweather is not expected. Showers and isolated thunderstorms will \nmove across the area Tuesday morning, continuing into the \nafternoon. The latest model guidance is considerably lower on the \ninstability late Tuesday afternoon and Tuesday night, generally \nwith CAPE values of 400 to 800 J/KG. 0-6km shear values of 30-40 \nknots. Bufkit soundings indicated low level winds of 30-40 knots \nTuesday night to support strong gusty winds with any storm. \nWetbulb zero heights fairly high, which is a concern for the \npotential for large hail. Main concern will be the ongoing showers\nand isolated storms Tuesday afternoon and how that will affect \nconvection into the evening. The latest Day 2 Outlook has a \nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for much of the area north and west \nof the Fox Valley. Showers and storms will come to an end late \nTuesday night. \n\nDry conditions expected Wednesday into Friday. The next chance\nof showers and storms at times will be from Friday night \nthrough the weekend. Confidence is low on timing and placement\nof convection due to the various model solutions. High\ntemperatures at or above normal through the weekend.",
